From f54b50b4380fe4e12ec285865009513de90196cd Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: tphuc <phuc.huynhthanh@avironactive.com>
Date: Wed, 28 Oct 2026 12:11:39 +0700
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] ntdll: let NtSuspendThread stop a sleeping thread on
 Horizon

A sleep on Horizon was a plain svcSleepThread, which a suspension cannot
interrupt: a garbage collector waiting for the thread's context then
waited as long as the sleep, forever for an infinite one. Sleep in the
server instead, where a suspended thread parks at once.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 dlls/ntdll/unix/sync.c | 12 +++++++-----
 1 file changed, 7 insertions(+), 5 deletions(-)

diff --git a/dlls/ntdll/unix/sync.c b/dlls/ntdll/unix/sync.c
index 43c5d67b..42fdf5c7 100644
--- a/dlls/ntdll/unix/sync.c
+++ b/dlls/ntdll/unix/sync.c
@@ -2492,11 +2492,13 @@ NTSTATUS WINAPI NtDelayExecution( BOOLEAN alertable, const LARGE_INTEGER *timeou
         extern int horizon_async_any_ready(void);
 
         /* wineserver interrupts a sleeping thread with a signal to run the
-         * socket operations that became ready for it. Horizon has no such
-         * signal: while one is ready, a sleep waits in the server instead,
-         * where it is handed over. The wait is not alertable, so no user APC
-         * runs, and it lasts as long as the sleep. */
-        if (horizon_async_any_ready())
+         * socket operations that became ready for it, or to suspend it.
+         * Horizon has no such signal, so a sleep waits in the server instead,
+         * where both are handled: NtSuspendThread then stops the thread in
+         * place rather than waiting for the sleep to end, which a garbage
+         * collector does not. The wait is not alertable, so no user APC runs,
+         * and it lasts as long as the sleep. A zero sleep only yields. */
+        if (horizon_async_any_ready() || !timeout || timeout->QuadPart)
         {
             if ((status = server_wait( NULL, 0, SELECT_INTERRUPTIBLE, timeout )) == STATUS_TIMEOUT)
                 status = STATUS_SUCCESS;
-- 
